{"reply":"In Part I, Chapter 10 of *The Story of My Life*, the final sentence is:

> “My disappointment was bitter at the time; but little by little I came to realize that it was not kind or wise to force this poor dumb creature out of his element, and after awhile I felt happy in the thought that perhaps he had returned to the sea.”

The change is from centering her own disappointment to considering what the crab needed—and then finding relief in its possible freedom. That doesn’t mean your wish to help your sister is wrong. But the passage offers a useful question: is an arrangement serving her sense of home, or mainly relieving your uncertainty about how things should be? What might change if you offered suggestions only after asking what she wants?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg2397-vb3cc1e13-sec012-ln1237-1254","source_location":"Part I, Chapter 10 — CHAPTER X, source lines 1237-1254","exact_quote":"My disappointment was bitter at the time; but little by little I came to realize that it was not kind or wise to force this poor dumb creature out of his element, and after awhile I felt happy in the thought that perhaps he had returned to the sea.","supported_claims":["“My disappointment was bitter at the time; but little by little I came to realize that it was not kind or wise to force this poor dumb creature out of his element, and after awhile I felt happy in the thought that perhaps he had returned to the sea.”","The change is from centering her own disappointment to considering what the crab needed—and then finding relief in its possible freedom."]}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}
